# PLAN-167 — ownership decision table. THIS FILE IS THE TRUTH.
# Reasoning lives in docs/ownership-decision-table.md; values live ONLY here.
# Conventions: "*" = don't-care (harness instantiates the canonical
# representative); "-" = not applicable under a §4 legality rule.
# note carries PROSE ONLY. Structured values live in columns (round-1 C1).
# `indistinguishable=` / `open=` remain annotations, never dimensions.
id	surface	prior_record	live_type	live_content	source_has	mode	ceremony	operation	skip_requested	fault	expect_verdict	expect_hash_source	origin	note
OWN-0001	spec	none	absent	-	yes	copy	maintainer	install_fresh	none	none	DELIVER	HASH_SOURCE	adr-155	indistinguishable=HASH_TARGET
66
   180	### 2.10 `fault` — the tenth dimension (ratified in round 1)
   181	
   182	`none` · `backup_unwritable`.
   183	
   184	An injected environmental failure. It is not a property of the target, which
   185	is why it is not `live_type`; it is a genuine tenth axis, and it rode inside
   186	the `note` column as a prose directive until the round-1 debate ruled that a
   187	**dimension the harness parses out of prose is a dimension nothing
   188	validates**.
   189	
   190	Dropping those rows was the lower-friction alternative and was rejected:
   191	they are the backup-failure *safety* cells, and a failed backup followed by a
   192	delete is the data-loss path the whole backup-before-replace contract exists
   193	to prevent. A column is cheap; a hole is not.
   194	
   195	Consequently `note` now carries **prose only**. `indistinguishable=` and
   196	`open=` survive as annotations because neither changes what the fixture does
   197	or what the decision function returns.
   198	
   199	## 3. The verdict enum (draft — W1 ratifies)
   200	
   201	The outcome of a cell is a **pair**. Every defect found in the eleven review
   202	rounds was a cell whose pair was wrong — which is the evidence that the pair
   203	is the right shape for the answer.
   204	
   205	### 3.1 First field — what happens to the target
   206	
   207	| Verdict | Meaning |
   208	|---|---|
   209	| `DELIVER` | write the framework's version; the target had nothing |
   210	| `REFRESH` | replace existing content, **backup first** |
   211	| `PRESERVE_OWNED` | do not touch the target; **keep** the delivery record |
   212	| `PRESERVE_UNOWNED` | do not touch the target; do **not** record (the adopter owns it) |
   213	| `OMIT_RECORD` | target stays; its record leaves the manifest |
   214	| `ABORT_SURFACE` | refuse this surface, rc 0, named warning; the run continues |
   215	
   216	`ABORT_SURFACE` is distinct from `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`: it is the outcome when
   217	the framework *wanted* to act and could not do so safely — a failed backup,
   218	an unsupported special file — and the distinction matters because the
   219	operator must be told.
   220	
   221	### 3.2 Second field — where the manifest's digest comes from
   222	
   223	| `hash_source` | Meaning |
   224	|---|---|
   225	| `HASH_TARGET` | hash the bytes now on disk at the target |
   226	| `HASH_SOURCE` | hash the framework's copy in `$SOURCE_DIR` |
   227	| `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` | carry the digest the previous manifest recorded |
   228	| `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` | the computed hash of what the pointer heredoc *would* generate |
   229	| `HASH_NONE` | emit no record |
   230	| `LINK_RECORD` | emit `LINK  <relpath>  <target>` instead of a digest |
   231	
   232	The two fields are **orthogonal**. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_TARGET`
   233	records the adopter's edited bytes as the framework baseline — which is how
   234	a later upgrade comes to overwrite them and `uninstall.sh` comes to delete
   235	them. `PRESERVE_OWNED` with `HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` is the safe reading of the
   236	same intent. Nothing in the branch structure made that choice visible; a
   237	column does.
   238	
   239	### 3.3 What this replaces
   240	
   241	`FMS_HASH_ROOT_PATHS` and `FMS_LINK_PATHS` are per-path override lists added
   242	during the eleven rounds to narrow two global switches that turned out to be
   243	too wide. They are **special cases of an explicit `hash_source`**, and the
   244	implementation replaces them — it does not keep them alongside it. Adding a
   245	third override list is the failure mode this table exists to prevent.
   246	
   247	`FMS_PROTOCOL_HASH` carries **two different meanings** today: the canonical
   248	pointer hash on the upgrade path, and a *prior record digest* on the install
   249	continuity path. Under this model those are `HASH_CANONICAL_POINTER` and
   250	`HASH_PRIOR_RECORD` — distinct values that must not share a channel. See
   251	OQ-4.
   252	
   253	### 3.4 `HASH_TARGET` is the default, and it is never distinctly correct
   254	
   255	Filling the table surfaced something no single branch could show: across all
   256	61 rows, **`HASH_TARGET` is never the right answer.** It appears only as an
   257	`indistinguishable=` annotation on rows where the target was just written
   258	from the framework's own bytes, so the two candidates are equal by
   259	construction and the distinction is unobservable.
   260	
   261	Yet `HASH_TARGET` is precisely what the generator falls back to when no
   262	override is supplied. So the default is right only by coincidence — whenever
   263	target and source agree — and wrong in exactly the situation the override
   264	exists to handle: a preserved adopter edit. Three separate P1 defects are
   265	instances of that one sentence.
   266	
   267	This is an argument for making `hash_source` an explicit, required parameter
   268	of the verdict rather than a set of opt-in overrides on a permissive default.
   269	Recorded as evidence for the W1 debate, not decided here.
   270	
   271	## 4. Legality rules (pruning)
   272	
   273	A cell is **illegal** when the combination cannot occur against a real
   274	target. Every rule below removes cells; **each is named with its reason, and
   275	silent pruning is forbidden** — an unexplained absence from the TSV is
   276	indistinguishable from an oversight, which is how a defect class hides.
   277	
   278	| Rule | Statement | Reason |
   279	|---|---|---|
   280	| **R-01** | `operation=install_fresh` ⇒ `prior_record=none` | The manifest is written at the *end* of install. "Fresh" is defined as "no pre-existing manifest", so there is no prior testimony to read. |
   320	  are independent. `mode=link ∧ prior_record=hash` is an open, unfixed defect
   321	  — a `--link` rerun over a copy-installed surface that has since been
   322	  replaced by a symlink, where the absence of a `LINK` row is read as "no
   323	  mismatch" and an arbitrary live symlink is recorded as a trusted delivery.
   324	  `mode=copy ∧ prior_record=link` is a legal re-run after a mode change.
   325	  Pruning either would delete the finding the table exists to hold.
   326	
   327	- **REJECTED: `surface=protocol ⇒ live_type ∈ {absent, regular, symlink}`.**
   328	  Nothing in `_refresh_protocol_pointer` prevents a directory or a FIFO at
   329	  `$TARGET/PROTOCOL.md`. It has neither the non-regular-destination guard nor
   330	  the leaf-symlink guard that `spec` and `marker` both acquired during the
   331	  review rounds — see §5.1. The cell is not illegal; it is **unguarded**, and
   332	  those are opposite things. Pruning it would have converted a live defect
   333	  into an invisible one.
   334	
   335	## 5. Cells whose pair is not obvious
   336	
   337	Only the reasoning lives here; the pairs themselves are in the TSV.
   338	
   339	### 5.1 The `protocol` surface is the family's late sibling
   340	
   341	`spec` and `marker` each acquired, over the review rounds, three guards: a
   342	symlinked-ancestor refusal, a leaf-symlink check validated against the prior
   343	`LINK` record, and a refusal of any destination that exists but is not a
   344	regular file. `protocol` acquired **none of them** — and it is the one
   345	surface written with `cat >`, which follows a leaf symlink out of the target
   346	tree, fails hard on a directory (aborting the whole run under
   347	`set -euo pipefail`, mid-upgrade, after other surfaces have changed), and
   348	blocks forever on a FIFO.
   349	
   350	Two of those three are real gaps. The third is not: R-11 shows the
   351	ancestor guard is **vacuous** for a root-level leaf, so its absence is
   352	correct rather than missing. That correction is itself the point — the same
   353	sweep that found the gaps also stopped a plausible-looking third "gap" from
   354	being patched into existence.
   355	
   356	None of this came from the eleven rounds. It came from asking the question
   357	the table forces: *the same cell, on each surface, must have a declared
   358	answer.* Per PLAN-167 §6 rule 2 it is recorded as rows, not patched
   359	branch-locally.
   360	
   361	### 5.4b The instrument was blind to the damage it was built to detect
   362	
   363	The first version of this harness compared only the target tree. For the
   364	rows where a surface is a symlink, that is not enough: a write that follows
   365	the link lands **outside** the target, on adopter or system data, and the
   366	target itself is unchanged. Such a row could report GREEN while the run
   367	destroyed a file the test never looked at.
   368	
   369	The fixture's foreign file is now a **tripwire**, digested before and after
   370	every run. Any change to it produces status `ESCAPE`, which outranks the
   371	verdict comparison entirely — a row whose pair matches while the run wrote
   372	out of tree has not passed.
   373	
   374	Arming it immediately converted a suspicion into evidence. `OWN-0034` — the
   375	`protocol` surface as a leaf symlink — reports `ESCAPE`: `cat >` follows
   376	the link and writes outside the target. `OWN-0044` (a `spec` symlink, which
   377	is correctly preserved) does not, so the tripwire is not simply firing on
   378	every symlink row.
   379	
   380	That promotes the §5.1 finding. The missing leaf-symlink guard on the
   381	pointer is not a hypothetical hardening gap; **it is a demonstrated
   382	out-of-tree write**, which is the S238 class the whole baseline-manifest
   383	design exists to close.
   384	
   385	### 5.4c `prior_record` is ambiguous, and it matters exactly where it hurts
   386	
   387	Running the decision function in shadow mode against the real callers — it
   388	observes and records, it does not act — produced 17 agreements, 2
   389	divergences and 10 rows the caller never reached. One divergence is a model
   390	defect, not an implementation defect.
   391	
   392	`prior_record` is defined as "what the pre-run baseline manifest says". There
   393	are **two** such manifests and the definition does not choose between them:
   394	
   395	- the **raw** file on disk, and
   396	- the **sanitized** one the loader produces, which drops every record whose
   397	  relpath traverses a symlinked component.
   398	
   399	They agree everywhere except on the symlink-traversal rows — which are the
   400	security-critical ones, and the same rows §5.8 is about. An observer reading
   401	the sanitized manifest sees `none` and concludes `PRESERVE_UNOWNED`; an
   402	observer reading the raw file sees `hash` and concludes `OMIT_RECORD`. Both
   403	are defensible readings of a dimension that never said which it meant.
   404	
   405	The resolution is not to pick the more convenient one. The **sanitized**
   406	manifest is the authority, because honouring a record whose path crosses a
   407	symlink is precisely what the ADR-155 decision-(v) provenance fence exists
   408	to prevent — but the definition in §2.2 has to say so, and the harness has
   409	to observe the same thing the caller does, or the two instruments will keep
   410	disagreeing about cells neither of them is wrong about.
   411	
   412	This is the kind of defect that survives eleven rounds of code review: every
   413	branch reads *a* manifest, each reads a defensible one, and no branch is
   414	individually wrong.
   415	
   416	### 5.4d The missing cell was the most important one
   417	
   418	The table had nine `protocol` rows and none for the combination that matters
   419	most: an **adopter-customised pointer on a normal (maintainer) upgrade**.
   420	`OWN-0072` covers the same content under `ceremony=user`; the ordinary path
   421	was simply absent.
   422	
   423	Deriving its expected pair exposed a **data-loss defect in the proposed
   424	decision function**. For that cell the function returned `REFRESH` — it would
   425	have overwritten a customised root `PROTOCOL.md`. That is the verified S238
   426	loss that ADR-155 decision (iii) exists to close, and the live code has
   427	preserved it correctly all along.
   428	
   429	The asymmetry the function was missing is deliberate and is now stated in it:
   430	
   431	| Surface | An adopter edit is… | Because |
   432	|---|---|---|
   433	| `SPEC/v1` | a **fork of the contract** → forced refresh | it is the published compliance contract (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §4) |
   434	| `PROTOCOL.md` | **adopter content** → preserved | overwriting it is the verified S238 loss (ADR-155 (iii)) |
   435	
   436	Both record a **canonical** digest regardless, because recording the
   437	customised bytes would make the *next* upgrade read `H_dst == H_base` and
   438	clobber them — the C.5 idempotency trap.
   439	
   440	Two things are worth separating here. The defect was in the **new** code, not
   441	the old: a refactor that had been driven only by "keep the map green" would
   442	have shipped it, because **no existing row covered the cell**. What found it
   443	was asking the completeness question — *which combinations does this surface
   444	have, and is each one present?* — which is the one question a per-branch
   445	review never asks.
   446	
   447	### 5.4e Install and upgrade generate DIFFERENT pointers — every upgrade breaks it
   448	
   449	Chasing a `HASH_UNCLASSIFIED` observation led to a defect in the live code
   450	that has nothing to do with ownership records.
   451	
   452	`install.sh` writes the root pointer and then **substitutes** its
   453	placeholders, so the adopter gets a real path. `upgrade.sh` regenerates the
   454	same file from a heredoc that leaves `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` **literal**, and
   455	nothing substitutes it afterwards. Verified against the live tree:
   456	
   457	| after | literal `{{PROTOCOL_SOURCE}}` occurrences |
   458	|---|---:|
   459	| `install.sh` | 0 — the file names the real checkout path |
   460	| `upgrade.sh` | 4 — the file names a placeholder |
   461	
   462	So **every upgrade degrades the pointer into a non-functional one** for any
   463	adopter whose framework checkout lives outside the target. The file whose
   464	entire job is to say where the protocol lives stops saying it.
   465	
   466	This is the **install-set ≠ upgrade-set** class that ADR-155 decision (i) was
   467	written to eliminate — the shared enumeration fixed *which paths* the two
   468	sides touch, but not *what content* they produce for the same path. It is
   469	pre-existing, unrelated to PLAN-167's changes, and no ownership assertion
   470	would ever have caught it: the record is fine, the bytes are wrong.
   471	
   472	It is recorded as **INV-4** because it is not a cell — it is a cross-writer
   473	invariant.
   474	
   475	### 5.5 Two findings are invariants, not cells
   476	
   477	Two defects were about the **blast radius** of a fix rather than about any
   478	one surface, and encoding them as rows would understate them. The harness
   479	asserts them across every applicable row instead:
   480	
   481	- **INV-1** — when ownership continuity fires on an install rerun, no
   482	  enumerated path *outside* the continuity set may change its recorded
   483	  digest. The original defect switched the whole tree's baseline to the
   484	  unrendered source, which reads downstream as repo-wide adopter drift and
   485	  silently stops those files from ever being refreshed again. One row could
   486	  not have caught it; the damage was to the paths the row was not about.
   487	- **INV-2** — `LINK` serialization may cover only paths that were already
   488	  `LINK` records before the run. Otherwise an adopter's own symlink,
   489	  preserved inside an enumerated directory, is promoted into a framework
   490	  delivery record.
   491	- **INV-4** — **install and upgrade must generate byte-identical content for
   492	  the same surface.** Violated today by the root pointer (§5.4e): install
   493	  substitutes its placeholders, upgrade does not. No per-cell ownership
   494	  assertion can see this — the record is correct and the bytes are wrong —
   495	  so it needs an invariant that compares the two writers' output directly.
   496	- **INV-3** — **an execution failure never advances the record.** A caller
   497	  that was handed `DELIVER` or `REFRESH` and could not complete it must
   498	  leave the manifest describing the world as it actually is. This is the
   499	  correction to the C2 proposal below: "the failure inherits the verdict's
   500	  `hash_source`" would record a delivery that did not happen — the framework
   501	  claiming bytes it never wrote, which is precisely the over-claiming
   502	  direction ADR-155-AMEND-1 §3 forbids. The failure path keeps the record
   503	  the surface had *before* the attempt.
   504	
   505	Both are the same shape: a switch that was correct in intent and too wide in
   506	scope. That is the single most common defect class in this space, and it is
   507	invisible to per-cell assertions by construction.
   508	
   509	### 5.2 Ownership continuity and the digest it carries
   510	
   511	Carrying ownership forward across a run that did not write anything is
   512	correct — dropping the record strands a delivered tree as unowned forever.
   513	But the flag and the digest are separate decisions, and four separate
   514	defects came from moving one without the other. A `PRESERVE_OWNED` verdict
   515	therefore always has an explicitly declared `hash_source`, and
   516	`HASH_TARGET` is never its default.
   517	
   518	### 5.3 Deliberate asymmetry: the marker under `--pin`
   519	
   520	Where a pinned pre-v1.3.0 source has no marker, the record is **dropped**
   521	rather than carried forward, so that readers fall back to `VERSION` — which
   522	the pin did update. This is the one place where `OMIT_RECORD` is chosen over
   523	`PRESERVE_OWNED` on purpose, and it is recorded here so it is not "fixed"
   524	into consistency with the other surfaces.
   525	
   526	Its residual is real and is open as OQ-3: `VERSION` in an *external* target
   527	is adopter-owned and never written by the upgrade, so the fallback can still
   528	report a version newer than the content.
   529	
   530	### 5.4 The root `VERSION` file is not a surface
   531	
   532	It is out of the enumeration and out of the upgrade, permanently and
   533	deliberately (ADR-155-AMEND-1 §2). It is named here only so that its absence
   534	from the table is not read as an omission.
   535	
   536	### 5.6 One amendment to the plan's observation contract, and why
   537	
   538	PLAN-167 §W0.3 drafted the observation contract so that `ABORT_SURFACE` was
   539	recognised by *"target unchanged + named warning + rc 0, and **no record**"*.
   540	Implementing it showed that clause is unsound: it makes the verdict field
   541	depend on the `hash_source` field, and §3.2 requires the two to be
   542	orthogonal. Under the draft rule, an `ABORT_SURFACE` that legitimately keeps
   543	a prior record would be observed as `PRESERVE_OWNED` — which is precisely
   544	the distinction OQ-2 exists to settle, silently pre-decided by the
   545	instrument that was supposed to measure it.
   546	
   547	The harness therefore recognises `ABORT_SURFACE` by the **refusal being
   548	emitted at all** — the framework attempted and declined — and reads the
   549	record independently. A refusal leaves no filesystem trace by construction,
   550	so this is matched against a small declared set of operator-visible markers.
   551	That coupling is deliberate and is the reason it is written down: if the
   552	wording changes, these rows fail, and they *should* — the operator-visible
   553	contract is part of what `ABORT_SURFACE` promises.
   554	
   555	The harness also emits `HASH_UNCLASSIFIED` when a recorded digest matches
   556	none of the four candidates. That is never an expected value: it means the
   557	manifest recorded something the model has no name for, which is a gap in the
   558	enum rather than a failing row.
   559	
   560	**`REFRESH` versus `PRESERVE` has no single sound signal.** When the incoming
   561	content equals what is already on disk, a refresh changes no bytes, and three
   562	candidate observables each fail somewhere:
   563	
   564	| Signal | Fails because |
   565	|---|---|
   566	| content digest | an identical-content refresh is invisible to it |
   567	| backup presence | the ADOPTER-FORK *preserve* path also snapshots — a backup proves the framework **looked**, not that it wrote |
   568	| modification time | `install.sh` re-runs placeholder substitution every invocation, rewriting the pointer with identical bytes and a fresh mtime — a write with no semantic content |
   569	
   570	The harness uses mtime, and **only on the upgrade path**, where the forced
   571	route is the one thing that rewrites these surfaces. The boundary is stated
   572	rather than assumed, because each of these signals looked universal until a
   573	row proved otherwise — and two of them were adopted, and withdrawn, during
   574	this work.
   575	
   576	The general lesson is worth more than the mechanism: in a space this shape,
   577	**the instrument needs the same adversarial scrutiny as the subject.** Five
   578	separate fixture defects were found while building this baseline, four of
   579	which produced a confident green or red that meant nothing.
   580	
   581	### 5.7 A guard placed on the right route, reached too late
   582	
   583	The `special`-file rows do not fail where they were expected to. The upgrade
   584	never reaches the SPEC or marker route at all: an earlier stage —
   585	`_emit_deprecation_warnings`, which shells out to a scanner that walks the
   586	whole target tree — opens the FIFO and blocks forever. The run dies before
   587	any surface is refreshed.
   588	
   589	Verified in isolation with both controls: the scanner alone, given a target
   590	whose only anomaly is a FIFO at the marker path, hangs indefinitely; the same

